          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The phrase denotes the extreme negative response or misery of the wicked under divine judgment and exclusion from God's kingdom, commonly understood as anguish, despair, or remorse, while hostile rage is a recognized alternative nuance.\",\"book_summary\":\"The phrase specifically denotes the wicked's hostile rage toward the righteous, especially when the wicked witness the kingdom from which they are excluded; grief and eventual destruction accompany that rage.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Extreme distress or misery experienced by the wicked in connection with divine judgment and exclusion.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Hostile rage of the excluded wicked directed toward the righteous.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book selects rage toward the righteous as the controlling meaning and thereby excludes readings in which gnashing primarily signifies remorse, passive anguish, physical torment, or generalized misery. It retains the consensus setting of wicked people reacting to judgment and exclusion.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no necessary contradiction with the broad consensus that the phrase expresses an extreme negative response to exclusion. However, the book materially reclassifies the specific emotion and its object: gnashing is active hatred toward the righteous, not chiefly anguish caused by conscious punishment.\",\"rationale\":\"The consensus itself recognizes rage or hostility as a common biblical interpretation, citing the same Psalms and Acts passage used by the book. Thus the book is not novel and does not replace the general judgment-and-exclusion referent. It narrows an emotionally broad consensus to one disputed component—hostile rage—and applies that component as the phrase's primary identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The Gospel formula repeatedly pairs gnashing with weeping, outer darkness, and fiery judgment, which may indicate comprehensive anguish rather than rage specifically. Moreover, hostile gnashing in Psalms and Acts occurs outside the fixed Gospel formula, so those parallels do not conclusively establish that every judgment occurrence has the same directed hostility.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
